From 313795f5abb862236de61cfbacb4637a381320e0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Thu, 23 Jul 2026 08:50:43 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] libpasswd: kill command injection in
 asus_libpasswd_openssl_crypt (C6 twin)

The C6 fix (8bc3189fa6) hardened shared/misc.c asus_openssl_crypt(), but an
independent, byte-identical copy still shipped in libpasswd:
asus_libpasswd_openssl_crypt() built "openssl passwd -%d -salt %s %s" with the
user-supplied password (key) and salt body and ran it via popen(), so a value
containing ; backtick $() etc. executed as root. It is reached from
compare_passwd_in_shadow() on the libc crypt()==NULL fallback -- i.e. from the
HTTP basic-auth check (httpd/web.c) and the lighttpd WebDAV/SMB basic-auth path
-- with the attacker-supplied password as the injected value.

Apply the same fix as C6: replace popen() with a local run_capture_line() helper
that runs openssl via an explicit argv (fork + execvp + pipe, no shell). No
behavioural change for legitimate inputs.

Reported to ASUS PSIRT as the C6 twin (case 1006563).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
